# SF Validation from Postman Output

This procedure is invoked by `run-regression-implementation.mdc` (Step 6) when
the user confirms they want Salesforce validation after a Newman run. It is
**not** triggered by a standalone phrase — it always receives two pre-resolved
variables from the caller:

- `newman_json_path` — full workspace-relative path to the Newman JSON report
  (e.g. `project/output_regression/sfl-crm-persona-sys-api-DEV-20260520-201820.json`)
- `api_name` — the API identifier derived from the collection filename
  (e.g. `sfl-crm-persona-sys-api`)

Read this file in full before executing any phase.

---

## Phase A — Parse Newman JSON and Extract Samples

Read the file at `newman_json_path`. The file is a Newman run report with a
top-level `run` key.

For each entry in `run.executions`:

1. **Filter** — keep only executions where:
   - `response.code` is in the range 200–299 (successful HTTP responses)
   - `request.body` exists and `request.body.raw` is a non-empty string
     (skip GET requests and requests with no body)

2. **Extract request payload**:
   ```javascript
   const reqPayload = JSON.parse(execution.request.body.raw);
   ```

3. **Extract response body**:
   ```javascript
   const respRaw = Buffer.from(execution.response.stream.data).toString('utf8');
   const respBody = JSON.parse(respRaw);  // may fail — treat as empty object if not valid JSON
   ```

4. **Extract Salesforce IDs from response body** — scan every key at the top
   level of `respBody` and any nested objects one level deep. A value is
   treated as a Salesforce ID candidate if it matches:
   - An 18-character alphanumeric string (standard SF ID format), **or**
   - A key whose name ends with `Id` or `id` (case-insensitive) and whose
     value is a non-empty string

   Collect these as `{ keyName: value }` pairs to merge into the sample.

5. **Compose the sample object**:
   ```json
   {
     "_instructions": "Generated from Newman run <newman_json_path>. Request name: '<item.name>'. SF entity IDs were extracted from the API response — verify they are valid before running the SF regression. If any ID shows as FILL_IN, locate it manually from the API response and paste it here.",
     "<sfIdKey>": "<extracted-value-or-FILL_IN>",
     ... spread all keys from reqPayload ...
   }
   ```
   - SF ID keys from the response take precedence over any same-named keys in
     the request payload.
   - If no SF IDs were found in the response, emit a comment in
     `_instructions` telling the user to fill them in manually.

6. **Slug the filename** — take `execution.item.name`, lowercase it, replace
   spaces and special characters with hyphens, trim to 60 chars. This becomes
   the sample filename: `{slug}.json`.

7. **Skip and report** — for executions skipped due to non-2xx status or
   missing body, print a one-line note in chat:
   `⚠ Skipped "{item.name}" (HTTP {code}) — no sample generated.`

At the end of Phase A, print a summary:
```
Extracted N sample(s) from Newman run:
  • {slug-1}.json  (from "{item.name}", HTTP 201)
  • {slug-2}.json  (from "{item.name}", HTTP 200)
  ⚠ Skipped: "{item.name}" (HTTP 400)
```

---

## Phase B — Resolve Codebase

Determine the Mule project folder for `api_name`:

1. **Auto-detect**: Check whether
   `project/output_develop/{api_name}/src/main/` exists in the workspace.
   - If yes, set `codebase_root = project/output_develop/{api_name}` and tell
     the user: `"Found Mule project at {codebase_root} — using it for codebase
     analysis."`

2. **Ask if not found**: If the auto-detect path does not exist, ask:
   > "Please provide the path to the Mule project for `{api_name}`
   > (the folder that contains `src/main/mule/` and `src/main/resources/`)."
   Set `codebase_root` to whatever the user provides. Accept both absolute
   paths and workspace-relative paths.

---

## Phase C — Analyse Codebase

Read the following files from `codebase_root` to build an understanding of
what Salesforce objects the Mule app writes and how it matches them:

### C1 — Constants (`config.properties`)

Read `{codebase_root}/src/main/resources/properties/config.properties`.

Collect every property that looks like a Salesforce literal:
- RecordType names (keys containing `RecordType`, `recordType`)
- Source system identifiers (keys containing `SourceSystem`, `source.system`)
- Status values, stage names, hard-coded labels

Build `consts_map = { propertyKey: propertyValue }`. These populate
`ruleset.consts` in Phase D.

If the file does not exist, skip and note in chat; `consts_map` will be empty
and the user should fill in `ruleset.consts` manually.

### C2 — Upsert transforms (`v-*.dwl`)

Glob `{codebase_root}/src/main/resources/dwls/v-*.dwl` and read each file.

For each DWL file:
- Infer the **Salesforce object** from the filename or the first line of the
  file (e.g. `v-lead-upsert-request.dwl` → `Lead`; look for comments like
  `// SF Object: Lead`).
- Extract **field mappings**: look for patterns like `FieldName: payload.X`,
  `FieldName: vars.X`, or `FieldName: "literal"`. Map each SF field to its
  source expression.
- Note whether the file is a **create**, **upsert**, or **update** operation
  (look for `sObject`, `upsert`, `create`, `update` keywords).

Build `sobject_map = { SObjectName: { dwl_file, fields: [{sfField, source}], mode } }`.

### C3 — Query SOQL (`p-set-*-queries.dwl`)

Glob `{codebase_root}/src/main/resources/dwls/p-set-*-queries.dwl` and read
each file.

For each file:
- Extract every SOQL SELECT statement, including its WHERE clause.
- Associate each query with the SObject it targets (FROM clause).
- These WHERE clauses are used **verbatim** (with template placeholders
  substituted) in `lookup.soql` in Phase D — this ensures the regression
  check replays the exact match logic the Mule app uses.

Update `sobject_map[SObjectName].match_soql` with the found WHERE clause.

### C4 — Flow XML (`src/main/mule/*.xml`)

Glob `{codebase_root}/src/main/mule/*.xml` and read each file.

For each XML file:
- Look for Salesforce connector operations: `<sfdc:create>`, `<sfdc:upsert>`,
  `<sfdc:update>`, `<sfdc:query>`.
- Extract the `type` attribute (SF object name) and the file/flow name.
- Use this to confirm or supplement `sobject_map` and to fill `cites:` blocks.

At the end of Phase C, print a compact summary:
```
Codebase analysis complete for {api_name}:
  • Config constants found: N
  • SF objects identified: Lead, Task, Account (from v-*.dwl)
  • SOQL match clauses found: Lead (from p-set-lead-queries.dwl), ...
  • Flow XML operations: {flow-file}.xml → sfdc:create Lead, sfdc:query Task
```

---

## Phase D — Write Artifacts

### D1 — Write sample files

Create directory `regression-tooling/{api_name}/samples/` if it does not exist.

For each sample composed in Phase A, write
`regression-tooling/{api_name}/samples/{slug}.json` with the sample object.

Do not overwrite existing files without asking:
> "A sample file `{slug}.json` already exists. Overwrite? (yes / no / rename)"

### D2 — Generate and write `ruleset.yaml`

Read `agents/regression/templates/ruleset.yaml` as the seed template.

Produce a new `ruleset.yaml` by substituting:

**Top-level fields:**
```yaml
ruleset:
  name: {api_name}
  api_version: v62.0
  default_org_alias: <<ask-user-if-not-inferrable>>
```

Ask for `default_org_alias` if it cannot be inferred from an existing
`regression-tooling/` folder or from a `.cursor/` config. Common values:
`insulet-devint2`, `insulet-devint`.

**`consts:` block:**
Populate from `consts_map` (Phase C1). Use the actual property values.
Comment out any entries where the value is unknown.

**`rules:` block:**
Generate one rule per SObject in `sobject_map` using the appropriate pattern:

| Situation | Pattern to use |
|---|---|
| SObject is always created (mode: create/upsert), match key is stable | Pattern 1 — simple positive lookup |
| Some fields only written when payload key is present (conditional DWL branch) | Pattern 2 — conditional fields with `when:` |
| SObject is a read-only lookup that may not exist (mode: query only, no create) | Pattern 3 — `allow_skip_when: always` |
| Match strategy depends on which payload keys are populated (multiple WHERE variants in SOQL) | Pattern 4 — multi-lookup fallback |
| Any field is a Salesforce picklist (detected by field name ending in `__c` or known picklist names like `Status`, `Gender`, `Type`) | Add `case_insensitive: true` to that field (Pattern 5 style) |
| Always add one rule at the end | Pattern 6 — `integration-error-absence` negative assertion |

For each rule:

- `id:` — slugify the SObject name (e.g. `Lead` → `lead`, `Task` → `task`,
  `Integration_Error__c` → `integration-error-absence`)
- `object:` — exact SObject API name from `sobject_map`
- `description:` — one sentence explaining what the Mule app does with this
  object, derived from Phase C analysis
- `cites:` — list the actual DWL/XML file paths found in Phase C for this
  object (e.g. `project/output_develop/{api_name}/src/main/resources/dwls/v-lead-upsert-request.dwl`)
- `lookup.soql` — use the WHERE clause from `match_soql` (Phase C3) with
  payload fields replaced by `{{ payload.X }}` templates. For objects where
  no query SOQL was found, construct a best-effort WHERE clause from the
  field mappings (e.g. `WHERE External_Id__c = '{{ payload.leadId }}'`)
- `expect.fields` — map each `{sfField}` from Phase C2 to its payload
  source as a template: `SFField: "{{ payload.payloadKey }}"`. For literal
  values (e.g. `"Contact Local TM"`), use `consts.KeyName` if the value
  appears in `consts_map`, otherwise inline the literal.

Add `depends_on: []` for Layer 0 (root) objects; add `depends_on: [<parent-id>]`
for objects whose match SOQL references a captured parent ID.

Set `capture_as` for the primary entity (the first created/upserted SObject
with a stable external ID). Do not set `capture_as` for negative-assertion
rules or purely read-only lookups.

**Important**: Label the generated ruleset clearly as a draft:

```yaml
# ============================================================================
# GENERATED DRAFT — review before running the SF regression
#
# Generated by: agents/regression/rules/sf-validation-from-postman.mdc
# Source Newman run: {newman_json_path}
# Codebase analysed: {codebase_root}
# Generated at: {ISO timestamp}
#
# Steps to validate this ruleset:
#   1. Check every `lookup.soql` WHERE clause matches the Mule app's
#      actual query logic in p-set-*-queries.dwl.
#   2. Verify `expect.fields` values match what the DWL writes, not just
#      what the payload contains.
#   3. Fill in any <<UNKNOWN>> placeholders.
#   4. Run: "regression test {api_name} against <org>"
# ============================================================================
```

Write to `regression-tooling/{api_name}/ruleset.yaml`.

If the file already exists, ask:
> "A `ruleset.yaml` already exists for `{api_name}`. Overwrite with the
> generated draft? (yes / no)"

### D3 — Print generation summary

```
Generated for {api_name}:

  regression-tooling/{api_name}/
  ├── ruleset.yaml       ← DRAFT — N rules across M SF objects
  └── samples/
      ├── {slug-1}.json
      └── {slug-2}.json

Review the ruleset before running, especially:
  • WHERE clauses in lookup.soql
  • Field values in expect.fields
  • Any <<UNKNOWN>> placeholders
```

---

## Phase E — Trigger SF Regression

Ask the user:
> "Which samples would you like to run the SF regression on?
> Reply **all** to run all N samples, or list the filenames
> (e.g. `{slug-1}.json, {slug-2}.json`)."

For each selected sample:

1. Read `agents/regression/templates/regression-validate.mdc` in full.
2. Execute the procedure defined there, passing:
   - `ruleset_path = regression-tooling/{api_name}/ruleset.yaml`
   - `payload = regression-tooling/{api_name}/samples/{selected}.json`
   - `org_alias` = `ruleset.default_org_alias` (from the just-written
     ruleset), or whatever the user provided if asked during Phase D
   - `report_template_path = agents/regression/templates/report-template.html`

Reports are persisted to:
```
project/output_regression/{api_name}-sf-validation/
  {run_id}-{slug}.md
  {run_id}-{slug}.html
```

After all selected samples have been run, print a combined one-liner:
```
SF validation complete. Samples run: N. Reports in project/output_regression/{api_name}-sf-validation/.
```

---

## Constraints and conventions

- **Never auto-fix the ruleset.** If a SOQL fails or a field assertion
  mismatches, surface it as a finding in the report — do not edit
  `ruleset.yaml` on your own.
- **Draft label is mandatory.** Every generated `ruleset.yaml` must carry
  the `GENERATED DRAFT` header comment defined in Phase D2.
- **Cite real paths.** Every `cites:` block must reference an actual file
  that was read during Phase C. Do not invent paths.
- **Sanitise PII in samples.** If the request body contains obvious PII
  (email, phone, name fields), replace the values with realistic-looking
  placeholders (e.g. `"test.user@example.com"`, `"555-0100"`) before
  writing the sample file.
- **One rule per SObject.** Bundle all field assertions for a given SObject
  into a single rule rather than spreading them across multiple rules.
- **`integration-error-absence` is always last.** Add it regardless of
  whether it was detected in the codebase — it is a universal post-condition
  for every Mule API.
